      "mathematics": {
        "verdict": "pass",
        "independent_solution": "Normalize 0.3333=33.33*10^-2. At row33/main3/correction3, the table root is 5.771+0.003=5.774. Rescale by 0.1 to 0.5774, then multiply by 6 to obtain 3.4644, reporting 3.464 at three decimals. Exact inward transfer gives sqrt12, whose captured entry is 3.464 directly. Both three-decimal reports are 3.464. Route2 has one main lookup but no correction or rescaling, so it requires fewer such operations. Equality of reports does not make 0.3333 exactly 1/3.",
        "correct": ["b"],
        "option_checks": [
          {"option_id": "a", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "3.4626=6*(5.771/10) omits the captured +0.003 correction. The required product is 3.4644, so route1's report is 3.464, not 3.463. The direct route2 value and convenience statement alone do not rescue this complete option."},
          {"option_id": "b", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "6*((5.771+0.003)/10)=3.4644, round3=3.464; row12/main0 returns3.464. Route2 omits route1's correction lookup/addition and decimal root rescaling, matching all components."},
          {"option_id": "c", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "0.34644=6*(5.774/100) applies the radicand's 10^-2 factor directly to the root instead of its 10^-1 square-root factor. Correct rescaling gives3.4644, not0.34644."},
          {"option_id": "d", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "The numerical pair is correct, but route1 needs a main lookup, correction retrieval/addition and rescaling, while route2 needs only the main entry. Route1 is not the route with fewer table/correction/rescaling operations."}
        ]
      }
